1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A sub-agent and the main-agent are similar, but they live in different zones.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The main-agent holds your whole conversation.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
A sub-agent starts fresh, does one job in isolation, and hands back a summary.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This lesson maps that line, shows how to define a sub-agent, and clears up what agent memory is.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's break it down.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's anchor three terms before we go deeper.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
First, a sub-agent.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
That's a separate, clawed code instance you spawn to handle one delegated subtask, and

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
it runs with its own tools and permissions in an isolated context.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Second, that isolated context.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
It means the sub-agent has its own context window.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
It doesn't see your conversation history or the files you've already opened.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
And when it's done, only a summary comes back to the main-agent.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Third, agent memory.

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
That's persistent state an agent keeps across sessions, living outside the live context window.

16
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
So it survives compaction and carries over from one session to the next.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Sub-agent, isolation, memory.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Hold on to those three.

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Let's zoom in on the one property that defines a sub-agent.

20
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
It gets a fresh, separate context window.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:16,000
That's the whole point.

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
It doesn't inherit your chat history, it doesn't see the skills you've already invoked, and

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
it doesn't know about the files Claude has read for you.

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,000
It starts clean.

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
It runs with its own system prompt, its own set of tools, and its own permissions, all

26
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
scoped to the job you handed it.

27
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
And when it finishes, it doesn't dump all its reasoning back into your conversation.

28
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
It returns a summary.

29
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Here's the mindset shift.

30
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
That isolation isn't a limitation to design around.

31
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
It's the reason to use a sub-agent in the first place, because it keeps your main context clean.

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
So how do you actually create one?

33
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
A sub-agent is a markdown file with YAML front matter at the top, and the body of that file

34
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
becomes the sub-agent's system prompt.

35
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Here's the simple part.

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Only two fields are required, a name and a description. That's it.

37
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Everything else is optional.

38
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
You can specify which tools it's allowed to use, which model it runs on, and what permissions

39
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
it has, but you don't have to.

40
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
If you leave the tools field out, the sub-agent just inherits the full set the main agent has.

41
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And that description does double duty.

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
It's not only a label, it's what Claude reads to decide, on its own, whether to hand a given

43
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
task off to this sub-agent.

44
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So write it to describe when the agent should be used.

45
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Let's put the two side by side, because seeing the contrast makes the isolation click.

46
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
The main agent is the conversation you're in right now.

47
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
It has the full picture.

48
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Everything you've said, every file it's read, every skill it's pulled in, it all lives in

49
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
that one context.

50
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
A sub-agent has none of that.

51
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
It starts from scratch, with only the task you delegate to it.

52
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
It works that task off in its own space, and then it reports back with a summary rather

53
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
than its full transcript.

54
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Why does that matter?

55
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Because all the messy intermediate work, the tool calls, the dead ends, the long outputs,

56
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
they stay out of your main context.

57
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Your conversation stays clean, and it stays focused.

58
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
There are three ways to put a sub-agent to work, and they escalate in how much control

59
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
you're handing over.

60
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
The first is just natural language.

61
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
You ask Claude to use a particular sub-agent, and it decides whether to delegate, based

62
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
on that sub-agent's description.

63
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The second is an at-mention.

64
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,000
You mention the agent by name, and that guarantees that specific sub-agent runs for the next task. No guessing.

65
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The third is a startup flag.

66
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
You launch with the agent flag set, and the entire session runs as that sub-agent from

67
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
the very first message.

68
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
So it's a spectrum.

69
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Suggest it, guarantee it for one task, or commit the whole session to it.

70
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Now, let's clear up the biggest point of confusion in this whole area.

71
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
People hear agent memory and think it's the same as Claude.md. It isn't.

72
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Think about who's doing the writing.

73
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Claude.md is you writing instructions for Claude to follow.

74
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
It's static, developer-authored rules.

75
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Agent memory is the reverse.

76
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
It's durable state the agent writes for itself, updating it as it works.

77
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
That difference is everything.

78
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
One is your standing guidance that you author and maintain.

79
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The other is the agent's own running notebook.

80
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And because memory lives outside the live context window, it survives compaction, and

81
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
it carries over after a session ends.

82
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
So don't file these two under one heading.

83
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
They're complementary, but they come from opposite directions.

84
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
There's one more layer here, and it's a common trap on its own.

85
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:03,000
When people say agent memory in Claude code, they can mean two different systems.

86
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
The first is main agent auto-memory.

87
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
This is Claude writing notes to itself, learning from your corrections and preferences over time.

88
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
It's on by default, and it's stored in a per-project folder on your own machine.

89
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
So it's local to you, not synced across machines.

90
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
The second is sub-agent memory.

91
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
That's a sub-agent's own private store, and you turn it on with a field in the sub-agent's front matter.

92
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
It lets that one agent build up knowledge across sessions on its own.

93
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Same word, memory, but two distinct mechanisms.

94
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Keep them apart, and the two systems stay easy to reason about.

95
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Let's tie this whole domain together with a decision you'll make constantly.

96
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
You've got a rule, a skill, and a sub-agent, and each one fits a different shape of problem.

97
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Ask yourself three quick questions.

98
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Is this an always-apply policy, something you want true on every turn?

99
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Then it's a rule.

100
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Is it an on-demand workflow, a procedure you only need sometimes?

101
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Then package it as a skill.

102
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Does it need to run in its own space, isolated from your main context?

103
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Then delegate it to a sub-agent.

104
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Rule, skill, sub-agent, policy, procedure, isolation.

105
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
When you match the mechanism to the actual job, your setup stays clean, your context

106
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
stays lean, and each piece does exactly what it's good at.

107
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And here's the bottom line.

108
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:37,000
A sub-agent runs a delegated task in its own isolated context, and only a summary comes back.

109
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
It matters because the isolation keeps your main conversation clean.

110
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Just remember, agent memory is the agent's own state, not your CloudMD file.

111
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
That covers sub-agents and agent memory in practice.

112
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
I'll see you in the next one.

